{
  "id": "astro-001",
  "title": "Why does the Moon seem to change shape?",
  "category": "astronomy",
  "subject_area": "Earth and Moon",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "If the Moon is always the same shape, why does it look like a crescent one night and a full circle another night?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "The Moon is always half lit by the Sun, and the part of that lit half we can see from Earth changes as the Moon orbits Earth.",
  "domain_of_validity": "The Moon as seen from Earth; lunar eclipses, when Earth's shadow falls on the Moon, are a separate event.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "The Moon does not make its own light. It shines because the Sun lights up one half of it, the half facing the Sun. As the Moon travels around Earth, we see more or less of that lit half. The Moon itself never changes shape. Only our view of it changes, from a thin crescent to a full circle and back again.",
    "level_2": "Moonlight is sunlight reflecting off the Moon's surface. The Sun always lights the half of the Moon that faces it, and the other half is dark. Depending on where the Moon is in its orbit, we see all, part or none of the lit half. This gives eight phases, from new moon to full moon and back. At new moon the side facing Earth is dark, and at full moon we see the whole lit side.",
    "level_3": "The Moon takes about 27.3 days to orbit Earth, but about 29.5 days to go from one new moon to the next because of how sunlight hits the Moon. The difference D = S - O is about 2.2 days, since S is about 29.5 days and O is about 27.3 days. The eight phases in order are new, waxing crescent, first quarter, waxing gibbous, full, waning gibbous, third quarter and waning crescent."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "Over a month the Moon goes through eight phases, one after the other.",
    "At new moon the side of the Moon facing Earth is dark, even though the Moon is still there."
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
